Subject: [EXPERIMENT] arm64: dts: qcom: glymur: poll SCMI instead of waiting for the doorbell

The PDP0/CPUCP firmware on the Snapdragon X2 Elite Extreme processes SCMI
protocol 0x13 (Performance) messages and writes correct replies into the SCMI
shmem, but never rings the mailbox doorbell for them. The kernel waits on an
interrupt that never arrives, every perf transfer hits -ETIMEDOUT, and
scmi-cpufreq probes -110. Cores stay pinned at boot clock and, because
cpufreq-cooling is created by the cpufreq driver, the machine ends up with no
CPU cooling device at all.

Measured 2026-07-30 on an ASUS Zenbook A16 (UX3607OA) by sending the same
messages both ways through SCMI RAW -- raw/message (interrupt) versus
raw/message_poll (polling, which reads the shmem status directly and bypasses
the doorbell):

  message                             poll mode        interrupt mode  doorbell
  0x10/0x1 BASE_PROTOCOL_ATTRIBUTES   status 0         status 0        +1
  0x13/0x0 PERF PROTOCOL_VERSION      answers, -4      hangs forever   +0
  0x13/0x1 PERF PROTOCOL_ATTRIBUTES   status 0         hangs forever   +0

Doorbell accounting is unambiguous: 8 messages sent in interrupt mode took
IRQ 156 from 9 to 16 -- one per reply, zero for the 0x13 messages.

The perf protocol itself is healthy: 0x13/0x1 returns status 0 with attributes
0x00020003, i.e. three performance domains, power in mW. This is a signalling
bug only, not a SKU without DVFS -- BASE_DISCOVER_LIST_PROTOCOLS explicitly
advertises 0x13.

arm,no-completion-irq is a documented upstream boolean on the arm,scmi node
(Documentation/devicetree/bindings/firmware/arm,scmi.yaml), consumed in
drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c. It sets cinfo->no_completion_irq, which
makes is_polling_enabled() true, which makes the core poll every transfer.
The mailbox transport already advertises .poll_done = mailbox_poll_done, and
polling is empirically proven to work on this box for both 0x10 and 0x13.

Status: ★ BOOT-TESTED AND PASSED 2026-07-31. Three performance domains came up,
scaling_driver = scmi, the apss_cpucp_mbox doorbell IRQ stayed flat at 0 on every CPU,
and cpufreq-cooling devices were created without further changes. Built and staged as
/boot/glymur/glymur-a16-merged-gpu-scmipoll.dtb; a dtc diff against the
currently-booted DTB is exactly this one added line.

If the property alone is not enough, the C-side equivalent is
.force_polling = true in scmi_mailbox_desc
(drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/transports/mailbox.c), which is currently unset.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur.dtsi
@@ -412,6 +412,18 @@
 			mbox-names = "tx", "rx";
 			shmem = <&cpu_scp_lpri1>, <&cpu_scp_lpri0>;

+			/*
+			 * The PDP0/CPUCP firmware writes correct replies for
+			 * protocol 0x13 (Performance) into the shmem but never
+			 * rings the mailbox doorbell for them, so every perf
+			 * transfer times out and scmi-cpufreq probes -110.
+			 * Measured 2026-07-30 via SCMI RAW: 0x13/0x1 returns
+			 * status 0 (3 perf domains) in polling mode and hangs
+			 * forever in interrupt mode, with zero doorbell IRQs.
+			 * Poll every transfer instead of waiting for the IRQ.
+			 */
+			arm,no-completion-irq;
+
 			#address-cells = <1>;
 			#size-cells = <0>;

